> 
> 3. Create a function to convert the matrix to the desired DataFrame format. Let's assume that `N`, `M`, and `O` are indexed with names like `RowName` and `ColumnName` for clarity.
> 
>    ```julia
>    function matrix_to_dataframe(matrix, row_names::Vector, column_names::Vector)
>        N, M, O = size(matrix)
>        data = reshape(matrix, N * M, O)
>        rows = repeat(row_names, inner=M)
>        cols = permutedims(repeat(column_names, outer=N))
>        
>        df = DataFrame(vcat(rows, cols, eachslice(data, dims=1)...), 
>                       [:RowName, :ColumnName, (1:O)...])
>        return df
>    end
>    ```
> 
> 4. Use this function with your matrix and corresponding row and column names.
> 
>    ```julia
>    # Example data
>    N, M, O = 3, 2, 4
>    matrix = rand(N, M, O)
>    row_names = ["A", "B", "C"]
>    column_names = ["X", "Y"]
> 
>    df = matrix_to_dataframe(matrix, row_names, column_names)
>    println(df)
